body:
Thank you for your contribution! Sorry I'm going to reject this as IMO, this seems way overkill and works around the core issue in a very intrusive way.

Since it's very easy to reproduce I tried getting a protocol dump myself, and it looks like it's not _really_ a race and more an oversight in the object lifecycle plan.

What I mean is: as of now, the activation token had kinda "its own life" but actually depended on a window, to the point that it included it as its user data. The solution is making it "part of the window" (as intended) and destroying the token along the window in the first place so that it does not then try to access garbage later.

As to why this happens, I wonder if it's because (most?) compositors skipped some argument checks when a token didn't have a surface set beforehand. Now it's set so a compositor tries to access it for... stuff? And goes "hey this thing doesn't exist!" and kicks the client out. At least that's my theory.

I am sorry it's kinda complicated. I wish I could explain it right now but I'm not up for it lately. I seriously need to document the object lifecycle and the whole architecture in general, somehow. I won't lie the whole LLM scraping thing kinda got rid of all my motivation.

Hopefully a protocol dump makes it easier to see:

```
[ 558614.592] {Default Queue}  -> xdg_popup#113.destroy()
[ 558614.606] {Default Queue}  -> wp_fractional_scale_v1#77.destroy()
[ 558614.620] {Default Queue}  -> wp_viewport#52.destroy()
[ 558614.633] {Default Queue}  -> xdg_surface#73.destroy()
[ 558614.646] {Default Queue}  -> wl_surface#122.destroy()
[ 558614.663] {Default Queue}  -> wl_display#1.sync(new id wl_callback#130)
[ 558614.680] {Default Queue} xdg_activation_token_v1#115.done("[STUFF]")
[ 558614.697] {Default Queue}  -> xdg_activation_v1#29.activate("[STUFF]", wl_surface#122)
[ 558614.714] {Default Queue}  -> xdg_activation_token_v1#115.destroy()

```

The token should be deleted along everything else IMO, this way a done will not be sent (or at the very least ignored I think, the Wayland object lifecycle is weird).

After doing so, you can see what I mean:

```
[1069108.591] {Default Queue}  -> xdg_popup#76.destroy()
[1069108.602] {Default Queue}  -> wp_fractional_scale_v1#69.destroy()
[1069108.613] {Default Queue}  -> wp_viewport#84.destroy()
[1069108.624] {Default Queue}  -> xdg_activation_token_v1#146.destroy()
[1069108.635] {Default Queue}  -> xdg_surface#73.destroy()
[1069108.644] {Default Queue}  -> wl_surface#70.destroy()
[1069108.656] {Default Queue}  -> wl_display#1.sync(new id wl_callback#121)
[1069108.667] {Default Queue} discarded xdg_activation_token_v1#146.done("[STUFF]")
```

And the protocol error doesn't happen anymore to me.

Sorry for taking over like this, I really hope I don't come as too rude, I have no idea how else to tackle this situation. I can make a PR if you don't mind, otherwise take a look at the following patch.

<details>
<summary>Patch</summary>

```diff
--- a/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/wayland_thread.cpp
+++ b/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/wayland_thread.cpp
@@ -3710,6 +3710,8 @@ void WaylandThread::_xdg_activation_token_on_done(void *data, struct xdg_activat
 	xdg_activation_v1_activate(ws->wayland_thread->registry.xdg_activation, token, ws->wl_surface);
 	xdg_activation_token_v1_destroy(xdg_activation_token);

+	ws->pending_xdg_activation_token = nullptr;
+
 	DEBUG_LOG_WAYLAND_THREAD(vformat("Received activation token and requested window activation."));
 }

@@ -4563,6 +4565,10 @@ void WaylandThread::window_destroy(DisplayServerEnums::WindowID p_window_id) {
 		wl_callback_destroy(ws.frame_callback);
 	}

+	if (ws.pending_xdg_activation_token) {
+		xdg_activation_token_v1_destroy(ws.pending_xdg_activation_token);
+	}
+
 	if (ws.xdg_surface) {
 		xdg_surface_destroy(ws.xdg_surface);
 	}
@@ -5289,17 +5295,22 @@ void WaylandThread::window_request_attention(DisplayServerEnums::WindowID p_wind
 	}

 	if (registry.xdg_activation) {
+		if (ws.pending_xdg_activation_token) {
+			xdg_activation_token_v1_destroy(ws.pending_xdg_activation_token);
+			ws.pending_xdg_activation_token = nullptr;
+		}
+
 		// Window attention requests are done through the XDG activation protocol.
-		xdg_activation_token_v1 *xdg_activation_token = xdg_activation_v1_get_activation_token(registry.xdg_activation);
+		ws.pending_xdg_activation_token = xdg_activation_v1_get_activation_token(registry.xdg_activation);
 		// Set additional data on activation token when we want strong attention
 		if (p_hint_strong_focus) {
-			xdg_activation_token_v1_set_serial(xdg_activation_token, last_serial, wl_seat_current);
+			xdg_activation_token_v1_set_serial(ws.pending_xdg_activation_token, last_serial, wl_seat_current);
 			if (focused_window) {
-				xdg_activation_token_v1_set_surface(xdg_activation_token, focused_window->wl_surface);
+				xdg_activation_token_v1_set_surface(ws.pending_xdg_activation_token, focused_window->wl_surface);
 			}
 		}
-		xdg_activation_token_v1_add_listener(xdg_activation_token, &xdg_activation_token_listener, &ws);
-		xdg_activation_token_v1_commit(xdg_activation_token);
+		xdg_activation_token_v1_add_listener(ws.pending_xdg_activation_token, &xdg_activation_token_listener, &ws);
+		xdg_activation_token_v1_commit(ws.pending_xdg_activation_token);
 	}
 }

diff --git a/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/wayland_thread.h b/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/wayland_thread.h
index 8da154cc7e..72dde06977 100644
--- a/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/wayland_thread.h
+++ b/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/wayland_thread.h
@@ -367,6 +367,8 @@ public:

 		struct zxdg_toplevel_decoration_v1 *xdg_toplevel_decoration = nullptr;

+		struct xdg_activation_token_v1 *pending_xdg_activation_token = nullptr;
+
 		struct zwp_idle_inhibitor_v1 *wp_idle_inhibitor = nullptr;

 #ifdef LIBDECOR_ENABLED
```

</details>

I got carried a bit as this was fun to look into for once. Actually it turned out to be of the tamest bugs I've seen lately compared to some of the crazy races that pop up every once in a while, especially when it comes to the embedder.
